   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "parts": [
    {
     "text": "Shabu-shabu (Japanese: しゃぶしゃぶ, romanized: shabushabu) is a Japanese nabemono hotpot dish of thinly sliced meat and vegetables boiled in water and served with dipping sauces. The term is onomatopoeic, derived from the sound – \"swish swish\" – emitted when the ingredients are stirred in the cooking pot. The food is cooked piece by piece by the diner at the table.  Shabu-shabu is generally more savory and less sweet than sukiyaki, another hot pot dish."
    }
